    "title": "Houston Rockets: How to watch playoff series vs. LA Lakers",
    "text": "The fourth playoff matchup between Kevin Durant and LeBron James will include a Game 3 that is a streaming exclusive during the first-round series between the Rockets and Lakers.Elizabeth Conley/Houston ChronicleThe NBA’s new TV deal that began this season will change the way Houston Rockets fans have watched the first round of the playoffs for decades.This year marks the first time every playoff game will be broadcast exclusively by national rightsholders, a change ushered in by the NBA’s 11-year, $76 billion media rights deals with ESPN/ABC, NBC/Peacock and Prime Video. This year’s postseason will include another first — requiring a streaming subscription to watch certain games.Get Digital Access and Stay Informed With Trusted Local News.Get Digital Access and Stay Informed With Trusted Local News.ONLY 25¢The schedule for the first four games of the Rockets' first-round series against the Los Angeles Lakers that begins Saturday sees Game 1 on ABC, Game 2 on NBC, Game 3 on Prime Video and Game 4 on NBC. The Prime Video games require a subscription to Amazon’s streaming service (a 30-day free trial is available). The streamer had an inauspicious postseason debut Tuesday night, when its stream of the Heat/Bobcats play-in game cut out for nearly two minutes during overtime.Article continues below this adThat broadcast lineup is a notable change from the past few decades. For several years, fans were able to watch the first round on the local teams' regional sports network, unless a game was carried by a national over-the-air network. Then beginning in the second round, the national networks took over. Years before that, RSNs carried local teams' games into the conference finals until the NBA’s national network partner took over the broadcasts. That was the case during the Rockets' championship runs in 1994 and 1995, with some games even airing locally on a pay-per-view basis.But Game 7 of last year’s Rockets-Warriors series marked the final playoff game aired by a team’s RSN for the foreseeable future, and perhaps ever. Space City Home Network, the Rockets' regional carrier, will still air postgame shows after the national networks' game broadcasts, but will not broadcast games, as veteran TV play-by-play announcer Craig Ackerman informed viewers during Sunday’s regular-season finale against Memphis.Last year’s series against Golden State featured five different national announcing crews over seven games and with multiple networks calling this year’s opening series, Rockets fans won’t get the continuity they’re used to with Ackerman, analyst Ryan Hollins and sideline reporter Vanessa Richardson.Reggie Miller and Jamal Crawford, the analysts on NBC’s No. 1 announcing team with play-by-play man Mike Tirico, said they could see a long series, even if many pundits are writing off the Lakers due to the injuries to Luka Doncic and Austin Reaves.Article continues below this ad“I think both teams could have potential pitfalls,” Miller said during a conference call with media this week. “That’s the beauty of a 4-5 match-up, because it really is a 50/50. These games, in these series, always go six or seven, because it’s that close.”The series doesn’t lack subplots, whether it’s the Rockets-Lakers playoff rivalry that goes back to the 1980s or veteran superstars Kevin Durant (Rockets) and LeBron James (Lakers), who’ll play each other for the fourth time in a postseason series. Durant’s teams won two of the first meetings, which came in the NBA Finals in 2012, 2017 and 2018.“Kevin Durant, we talk about him as a scorer,” Crawford said. “We need to start talking about him as an all-time, all-around great because he can do every single thing on the court. And he does it in such a way that doesn’t disrupt the flow of anybody else. And it’s so special to watch. At this stage in his career, still to be this great and this elite, still be one of the very best players in the league, his team believes they have a chance. The way they defend, the way they guard, the Rockets could be a tough out as well.“LeBron… we just watched him in Dallas. They lost that game, but just seeing the bop in his steps, seeing how he was managing the game, I’m not saying that he is happy that his two other best players are out. But he has something in him, like he’s almost trying to prove something. He’s trying to prove that he can still hold down the fort and he can still bring guys along.”Miller said a question mark for him regarding the Rockets was how they performed in close games. In games with a margin of five points or fewer during the final five minutes this season, Houston went 13-23.“For Houston, if you just look at their crunch-time numbers, if the game is close, I just feel there’s way too much isolation and waiting for Kevin Durant to bail us out,” Miller said. “There’s got to be a little bit more flow to their offense, especially in playoff basketball, where there’s not a lot of fast breaks, where it’s more of a half-court game.“You’ve got to be able to have options other than your 1A and 1B, which is going to be KD. Other guys are going to have to be able to make plays.”April 15, 2026Weekend Sports Editor, Houston ChronicleGreg Rajan is the Weekend Sports Editor at the Houston Chronicle. He can be reached at greg.rajan@houstonchronicle.com. He also curates the Daily Playbook sports newsletter and writes about sports media. He joined the Chronicle in January 2015 and has worked in a variety of roles at the newspaper. He previously worked as the sports editor at the Corpus Christi Caller-Times. He led that sports section to three Associated Press Sports Editors top-10 national awards and his work has been honored by APSE and the Texas Associated Press Managing Editors. He also has worked at the Austin American-Statesman and Temple Daily Telegram. He is a graduate of Southwest Texas State University and Bowie High School in Austin.",
